INT. BOXING RING - CORNER - NIGHT

Round nine bell. Roaring crowd. Under the hot lights the ring floor is slick with sweat.

TRAINER DOMINIC "DOMMY" ESPINOZA, 60s, in a satin jacket with a towel around his neck, kneels at the stool. He sponges a fighter's face. In the opposite corner, a mirror image: another trainer, other fighter. They're not part of this scene.

KWAME ASANTE, 30s, the fighter, in red trunks, sits on the stool. One eye swollen shut, mouthguard in his hand. He breathes like a bellows.

DOMMY
Listen to me.

KWAME
I'm listening.

DOMMY
You're losing.

KWAME
I know.

DOMMY
Three rounds to go. You are down by two on every card.

KWAME
I know, Dommy.

DOMMY
(to the crowd, over his shoulder)
Give us room, please! Let him breathe!

KWAME
They can't hear you.

DOMMY
(to himself)
Of course they can't. Nobody listens to a cornerman.

KWAME
Dommy.

DOMMY
What?

KWAME
I can't see out of the left.

DOMMY
That's fine. The left is lazy anyway.

KWAME
You're not funny.

DOMMY
I'm trying to be.

He wipes blood from Kwame's brow. Jams a cotton swab into the cut. Kwame winces.

DOMMY (CONT'D)
Here's what you do. He's dropping his right on the exit. Every time. You see it?

KWAME
I see it.

DOMMY
When he drops it, you go over the top. One shot.

KWAME
One shot.

DOMMY
You've got one shot left in you, Kwame. Maybe two. I'll take the one.

KWAME
What if I miss?

DOMMY
Then we go home, and I buy you the biggest steak in the state.

KWAME ^
And if I don't miss?

DOMMY
Then I buy you two.

Kwame laughs. Spits red into the bucket.

DOMMY (CONT'D)
(to the whole arena, though no one hears)
This is my fighter!

The bell sounds.

KWAME
Thanks, Dommy.

DOMMY
Go.

Kwame stands. Jams in the mouthguard. Goes.
